"1. Load MovieLens100K ratings from the local `u.data` file in the current working directory using the correct tab-separated schema: user id, item id, rating, timestamp.\n2. Use LensKit\u2019s ImplicitMF recommender with a fixed configuration for all runs; only the random seed may vary across repeated trainings.\n3. Create a single 80/20 train/test split of the ratings data and keep that split identical across all seed runs so seed effects are isolated to model randomness, not data splitting randomness.\n4. Ensure all other stochastic sources are controlled or fixed where possible (e.g., split seed, evaluation candidate generation, NumPy/Python random state if relevant) so the experiment measures training-seed variability.\n5. Train the model multiple times with different random seeds; use enough runs to estimate variability meaningfully (at least 10 distinct seeds, preferably more).\n6. Generate top-N recommendations for each test user using the same recommendation cutoff `N` in every run.\n7. Evaluate each run with ranking metrics appropriate to the task, including at minimum NDCG and Recall; compute metrics consistently on the same test users/items across runs.\n8. Store per-run results in a structured table containing at least: seed, metric values, and any key run settings needed for reproducibility.\n9. Summarize seed sensitivity quantitatively across runs using statistics such as mean, standard deviation, min, max, and coefficient of variation or confidence intervals for each metric.\n10. Report the absolute and relative spread of metric values across seeds and identify whether differences are practically noticeable compared with the mean performance.\n11. Include basic validation checks so the script fails clearly if `u.data` is missing, malformed, or if evaluation produces empty user sets or NaN metrics.\n12. Make the experiment fully reproducible by explicitly recording the train/test split procedure, all model hyperparameters, all seeds used, package versions if available, and the exact evaluation protocol.\n13. Present a concise conclusion from the observed metric variability addressing the research question of whether random seeds materially affect results enough that reporting seeds is important."